1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,531
Here's what a single successful test doesn't tell you.

2
00:00:03,531 --> 00:00:07,654
It doesn't tell you what happens when that winner eventually fatigues.

3
00:00:07,654 --> 00:00:12,483
It doesn't tell you whether a fresh angle is ready to test next, or whether you'll

4
00:00:12,483 --> 00:00:16,606
be starting from a blank angle map the week fatigue actually shows up.

5
00:00:16,606 --> 00:00:21,318
Every skill this course has built so far works on one batch at a time: brief it,

6
00:00:21,318 --> 00:00:23,969
generate it, pre-flight it, test it, read it.

7
00:00:23,969 --> 00:00:27,091
None of those lectures said how often to do it again.

8
00:00:27,091 --> 00:00:31,685
That's this lecture's entire job: turning a one-time project into a rhythm the

9
00:00:31,685 --> 00:00:35,631
account runs on, whether or not this week happens to be a busy one.

10
00:00:35,631 --> 00:00:40,284
Without that rhythm, Section 8.4's iterate-or-new-angle call becomes a scramble

11
00:00:40,284 --> 00:00:41,580
instead of a decision.

12
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:46,469
A refresh built calmly, in advance, because the sprint scheduled it, is a different

13
00:00:46,469 --> 00:00:51,240
thing entirely from one built in a rush the day a winner's numbers finally crack.

14
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:54,597
Harlan's account is a useful example of this gap closing.

15
00:00:54,597 --> 00:00:59,486
The angle that won its first small test no longer runs on twenty-five dollars a day

16
00:00:59,486 --> 00:01:00,782
in an isolated ad set.

17
00:01:00,782 --> 00:01:05,611
It's scaled into real budget, and the sprint you're about to build keeps a refresh

18
00:01:05,611 --> 00:01:10,264
ready for it before fatigue forces a scramble, not a system that only exists on

19
00:01:10,264 --> 00:01:12,679
paper until the day it's actually needed.

20
00:01:13,229 --> 00:01:14,260
Notice the order.

21
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:18,323
Scoring comes first, every week, before any new creative gets made.

22
00:01:18,323 --> 00:01:23,052
Generating first and scoring later is exactly the taste-picks-the-winner habit

23
00:01:23,052 --> 00:01:27,964
Section 0 broke, just moved a few months downstream and dressed up as a schedule.

24
00:01:27,964 --> 00:01:33,057
Thursday's pre-flight step isn't optional just because Wednesday's batch came from a

25
00:01:33,057 --> 00:01:33,845
proven brief.

26
00:01:33,845 --> 00:01:38,817
Section 6's checklist exists precisely because speed is when a claim slips through

27
00:01:38,817 --> 00:01:43,607
or a licence gap goes unnoticed, and a weekly sprint moving fast is exactly the

28
00:01:43,607 --> 00:01:45,972
condition that checklist was built for.

29
00:01:45,972 --> 00:01:50,944
This rhythm doesn't require a full week's worth of new creative every single week.

30
00:01:50,944 --> 00:01:55,856
Some weeks, Monday's scoring turns up nothing that needs a refresh, and Wednesday

31
00:01:55,856 --> 00:01:57,978
through Friday are genuinely quiet.

32
00:01:57,978 --> 00:02:01,980
That's the sprint working correctly, not a sign it's been skipped.

33
00:02:01,980 --> 00:02:06,588
The schedule exists so you notice when something does need attention, not to

34
00:02:06,588 --> 00:02:09,498
manufacture busywork on weeks when nothing does.

35
00:02:09,498 --> 00:02:14,592
Why does the order matter this much, rather than just the presence of all five steps

36
00:02:14,592 --> 00:02:15,926
somewhere in the week?

37
00:02:15,926 --> 00:02:20,291
Because each step depends on the one before it having actually happened.

38
00:02:20,291 --> 00:02:23,808
Generating before scoring means guessing at what's needed.

39
00:02:23,808 --> 00:02:28,417
Launching before pre-flight means shipping whatever was made instead of what

40
00:02:28,417 --> 00:02:29,447
actually cleared.

41
00:02:29,447 --> 00:02:34,419
The order isn't a style preference, it's what makes each step's output the correct

42
00:02:34,419 --> 00:02:35,814
input for the next one.

43
00:02:36,364 --> 00:02:41,378
Five steps, and every one of them already exists somewhere earlier in this course.

44
00:02:41,378 --> 00:02:43,457
Nothing here is new work to learn.

45
00:02:43,457 --> 00:02:48,410
The runbook's entire value is the order and the cadence, not any individual step.

46
00:02:48,410 --> 00:02:53,301
Fill in your own weekday mapping if Monday through Friday doesn't match how your

47
00:02:53,301 --> 00:02:54,463
week actually runs.

48
00:02:54,463 --> 00:02:59,416
What matters is that scoring always comes first in the cycle and launching always

49
00:02:59,416 --> 00:03:02,168
comes last, whatever days you attach to them.

50
00:03:02,168 --> 00:03:07,304
Notice, too, that step three says "only what Monday and Tuesday actually require." A

51
00:03:07,304 --> 00:03:12,379
sprint that generates a fixed quota of new creative every week, whether or not last

52
00:03:12,379 --> 00:03:17,393
week's scoring called for it, is optimising for busyness, not for what the account

53
00:03:17,393 --> 00:03:18,310
actually needs.

54
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:23,324
Print this, pin it, or keep it wherever you'd actually see it on a Monday morning.

55
00:03:23,324 --> 00:03:28,460
A runbook that lives in a folder nobody opens is worth exactly as much as no runbook

56
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:28,888
at all.

57
00:03:28,888 --> 00:03:34,025
The five steps only do their job if something makes you look at them at the start of

58
00:03:34,025 --> 00:03:35,125
every single week.

59
00:03:35,675 --> 00:03:40,640
One honest trap worth naming, because it's the kind that only shows up months later.

60
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:45,605
A quiet Monday, nothing scored, nothing to refresh, feels like nothing happened, and

61
00:03:45,605 --> 00:03:50,392
it's tempting to skip the check-in entirely next week since this week didn't need

62
00:03:50,392 --> 00:03:50,570
it.

63
00:03:50,570 --> 00:03:53,820
Do that a few times and the sprint stops being a habit.

64
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:58,726
It becomes something you do only when a problem's already visible, which is exactly

65
00:03:58,726 --> 00:04:02,154
the reactive posture this whole section exists to prevent.

66
00:04:02,154 --> 00:04:06,587
Run the scoring step every week regardless, even on the weeks you're fairly

67
00:04:06,587 --> 00:04:08,242
confident nothing's changed.

68
00:04:08,242 --> 00:04:12,911
A five-minute check that confirms nothing needs doing costs you almost nothing.

69
00:04:12,911 --> 00:04:17,876
Losing the habit entirely, and rebuilding it from scratch the week a winner actually

70
00:04:17,876 --> 00:04:20,240
goes tired, costs you considerably more.

71
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:24,850
Ask yourself honestly, at the end of each quiet week: did you actually run the

72
00:04:24,850 --> 00:04:28,751
check, or did you just feel confident enough to assume the answer?

73
00:04:28,751 --> 00:04:33,420
Those are not the same thing, and only one of them is the habit this lecture is

74
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:34,543
asking you to keep.

75
00:04:35,093 --> 00:04:39,720
Next lecture puts real numbers against this rhythm: what a week of generation

76
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:43,386
actually costs, set against the media budget it's protecting.

77
00:04:43,386 --> 00:04:47,232
Before that, sketch out your own five-day version, even roughly.

78
00:04:47,232 --> 00:04:52,280
The exact days matter less than the order: score first, decide second, generate only

79
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:54,503
what's needed, check it, then launch.

80
00:04:54,503 --> 00:04:59,250
Get that order right and the schedule underneath it can flex to fit your actual

81
00:04:59,250 --> 00:04:59,551
week.

82
00:04:59,551 --> 00:05:04,599
Notice what this rhythm quietly assumes, too: that scoring, deciding, and generating

83
00:05:04,599 --> 00:05:09,526
are each somebody's actual job on a given day, not a vague intention floating over

84
00:05:09,526 --> 00:05:10,427
the whole week.

85
00:05:10,427 --> 00:05:15,355
If nobody owns Monday's scoring step specifically, it's the first thing that slips

86
00:05:15,355 --> 00:05:17,278
when a busier priority shows up.

87
00:05:17,278 --> 00:05:22,326
Section 9.3 picks up exactly that question next, who runs which day, once the rhythm

88
00:05:22,326 --> 00:05:23,407
itself is settled.
